course: dictionary
lesson: 2466
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it show you which meaning fits. Darling is a good example. «Come here, darling.» Here darling is a loving word for someone you love.
explain: «What a darling little puppy!» Here darling means very sweet or lovely. Test it by swapping in another word: "What a sweet little puppy!" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In some shops in parts of the UK, a friendly assistant may say «There you go, darling.» Here it is just a friendly way to speak, not a sign of deep love. Older people may call anyone darling to sound warm.
explain: Tone and the rest of the sentence can even turn the meaning around. «"Little darling" said ironically means a badly behaved child.» Ironically means the speaker means the opposite of the words, so you must read the whole sentence to know.
fact: Darling can be a noun or an adjective.
fact: Darling can be a loving word for someone you love, as in «Come here, darling.»
fact: A darling is a person who is very loved or favoured, like the darling of the crowd.
fact: As an adjective, darling means very sweet or lovely, as in «What a darling little puppy!»
fact: Words can change meaning over time: in the 1500s, «bully» meant darling or sweetheart.
exercise: «Come here, darling.» What does darling show? | *A loving feeling for someone | Anger at someone | A rule for school | A kind of weather
exercise: «What a darling little puppy!» What does darling mean here? | *Very sweet or lovely | A kind of biscuit | Very angry | Too big to fit
exercise: «"Little darling" said ironically means a badly behaved child.» What does the speaker really mean? | A very good, helpful child | A badly behaved child | A small, cute baby | *A badly behaved child, said as the opposite of what the words say
test: «There you go, darling.» is said by a friendly shop assistant. What does darling mean here? | A cross command | *A friendly way to speak to someone | A type of shop | Someone who is loved very much
test: A darling is a person who is very loved or favoured. What is the darling of the crowd? | *A very popular, much-loved person | A tired runner | A person who is lost | A noisy drum
exam: Which sentence uses darling as an adjective? | *What a darling little puppy! | Come here, darling | There you go, darling | Darling, please wait for me
